LeetCode 98 — Validate Binary Search Tree
1. Problem Statement

Given the root of a binary tree, determine whether it is a valid Binary Search Tree (BST).

For a valid BST:

left subtree values < node value < right subtree values

This rule must be true for every node.

Example
        5
       / \
      3   7
     / \ / \
    2  4 6  8

This is a valid BST ✅

2. 🧠 Intuition

A common mistake is to check only the immediate children.

For example:

        5
       / \
      3   7
     /
    6

6 < 5 is false, so 6 cannot be anywhere inside the left subtree of 5.

Therefore, when we move down the tree, we need to remember:

What is the allowed range for the current node?

Initially, any value is allowed:

(-∞, +∞)

For the root 5:

left subtree → (-∞, 5)
right subtree → (5, +∞)

Then if we go into the left child 3:

3's allowed range = (-∞, 5)

If we go into its right child:

allowed range = (3, 5)

So every node gets a valid range


Logic :

To validate a BST, I maintain a valid range for every node.
Initially, the root can have any value, so its range is negative infinity to positive infinity. 
When I move to the left subtree, the current node becomes the upper bound because all left values must be smaller.
When I move to the right subtree, the current node becomes the lower bound because all right values must be larger.
If any node falls outside its allowed range, I return false. If I reach null for every path, the tree is a valid BST.

Code :


class Solution {
    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        return valid(root,Long.MAX_VALUE,Long.MIN_VALUE);
    }
    boolean valid(TreeNode node,long max,long min){
        if(node==null){
            return true;
        }
        if(node.val<=min  || node.val>=max){
            return false;
        }
        return valid(node.left,node.val,min) && valid(node.right,max,node.val);
    }
}
